[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Managing Asynchronous Code and Avoiding Anti-Patterns in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cypress operates differently than traditional Selenium-based frameworks. Instead of traditional async/await mechanics, Cypress queues commands to be executed later. Understanding how to manage this command queue and avoiding common anti-patterns is crucial for building stable, reliable test suites."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Cypress Command Queue"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every Cypress command is an asynchronous promise-like instruction that gets added to a global queue. When you write standard JavaScript variables inside your test blocks, they often evaluate before the Cypress queue finishes executing. This leads to timing issues and flaky tests if not handled correctly."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern 1: Mixing Synchronous JS with Asynchronous Commands"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common mistake is trying to save the text of an element into a standard JavaScript variable and using it outside of the Cypress chain. Because the command hasn't run yet when the variable is declared, it will remain undefined."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// BAD: text is undefined because cy.get() is asynchronous
let text;
cy.get('.my-element').then(($el) => {
  text = $el.text();
});
console.log(text); // Logs undefined immediately"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Solution: Using .then() and Aliases"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To correctly manage values yielded by Cypress commands, keep your logic inside the .then() block or use the .as() alias command to share state across your test steps."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// GOOD: Keeping execution in the promise chain
cy.get('.my-element')
  .invoke('text')
  .then((text) => {
    cy.get('.other-element').should('have.text', text);
  });"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern 2: The 'Length' Property and Automatic Retrying"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Developers often try to write conditional statements based on the length of a retrieved element list. Standard JavaScript assertions do not retry, which breaks Cypress's automatic retry-ability."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// BAD: This will not retry if elements take time to load
const count = Cypress.$('.item').length;
if (count > 0) {
  cy.get('.item').click();
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Solution: Relying on Built-in Assertions"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Always use Cypress assertions like should('have.length.greaterThan', 0) to allow the test runner to automatically wait and retry until the condition is met."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// GOOD: Cypress automatically retries this assertion
cy.get('.item').should('have.length.greaterThan', 0).first().click();"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices Summary"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never use native async/await keywords with Cypress commands, as Cypress uses its own custom command queue."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use .then() to bridge asynchronous steps when you need to manipulate DOM data inside JavaScript."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid standard JavaScript if/else statements for checking application states; use built-in assertions instead."}]
